WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
##

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.166
##

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.208
STEINFELD: Oh, my God,
she's so insane.

00:00:13.917 --> 00:00:16.000
Of course, she's insane,
she's Emily Dickinson.

00:00:16.291 --> 00:00:17.333
##

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:21.166
Emily Dickinson, who is not
only an incredible writer,

00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:23.250
but just a fascinating person--

00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:25.542
-Oh, yeah, I'm a real catch.
-Sit like a lady.

00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:28.959
The interesting thing I would
say about this show

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:31.583
is that we're retelling
the story of Emily Dickinson

00:00:33.625 --> 00:00:35.959
in a way that's fresh and cool
and a little bit dangerous.

00:00:38.166 --> 00:00:39.208
##

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.542
I'm in love with Death.

00:00:42.959 --> 00:00:44.959
He takes me out for
a carriage ride every night.

00:00:44.875 --> 00:00:45.917
He's such a gentleman.

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:47.708
You are such a weirdo.

00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:50.125
If she was a normal girl,

00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:53.125
this would be a normal
coming-of-age female story.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.834
-[wretches]
-But she's Emily,

00:00:55.792 --> 00:00:57.208
so it's just not
what you expect.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:58.792
Why can't I go to college?

00:00:59.875 --> 00:01:01.208
I wrote about out it
in my essay,

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:02.625
"On the Proper Place of Women."

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.959
We can start really kind of
leaning into how ridiculous

00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:07.375
the time period is
in some ways,

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.750
and how ridiculous it is
that it feels relevant

00:01:10.208 --> 00:01:11.750
-today in a lot of ways.
-[laughing]

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.708
I mean, at least we get
to take our corsets off.

00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:16.375
This story has
an astonishing amount

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.500
of parallels to our
21st-century everything.

00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:22.250
My generation can watch
this show and feel

00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:24.542
like the 1800s
don't feel so far away.

00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:27.000
You thinking
what I'm thinking?

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:28.417
Yeah.

00:01:29.041 --> 00:01:30.083
Boys are stupid.

00:01:31.041 --> 00:01:33.333
That's what's been so fun,
is just playing the reality

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.500
of what it feels like
it would be to us today.

00:01:36.458 --> 00:01:38.417
We're always in a bit
of a metaphoric history.

00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:41.500
Elena took Emily's work
and made her

00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:44.000
a modern woman that we
could all relate to today.

00:01:43.917 --> 00:01:45.917
WOMAN: Kissing? My God,
what is wrong with you?

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.875
Her whole reasoning is,
we're playing human beings

00:01:49.458 --> 00:01:51.125
that just existed
at a different time.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.583
It doesn't mean
they didn't have fun.

00:01:52.542 --> 00:01:53.583
We need to throw one of our

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.708
classic Dickinson house parties.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.208
There's a dance sequence.
Watching everybody

00:01:58.875 --> 00:02:00.708
going from, like,
a traditional line dance

00:02:01.542 --> 00:02:02.583
into a twerk...

00:02:03.792 --> 00:02:05.333
is the most crazy thing
to be part of.

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:09.750
I think young people today
maybe don't have

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.792
the same need to put
themselves in boxes--

00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:15.166
And a kind of redefinition that
Emily would really approve of.

00:02:16.875 --> 00:02:18.291
I have one purpose
on this earth,

00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:22.041
and that is to become
a great writer.

